// Track-0 WORK-STEALING torture program (W212) — maximally UNEVEN per-P load.
//
// Why this exists: `alloc-torture` and `remote-free-torture` spread their work
// across processors by construction — main spawns every task itself, so each
// lands on a run queue somebody is already looking at, and a global FIFO would
// serve them just as well. Neither can tell a stealing scheduler from a fair
// one. This program can, because ALL of its work is created by ONE green
// thread and therefore enters ONE queue: every other P starts with an empty ring
// and an empty global queue, so the only way it can ever run anything is
// `__sched_find_runnable`'s stealing rounds. ⚠ Since EC10 that queue is the
// owner's COROUTINE queue and a thief cannot reach it at all — see the note
// below, which is the reading rather than a caveat on it.
//
// Mechanism: main spawns a single `producer`, which spawns `childCount`
// short-lived children onto whichever P is running IT and only then awaits any
// of them. The backlog is what wakes the other Ms (`__sched_wake_or_spawn`),
// and what they find when they look is a peer holding hundreds of runnable
// threads. `__Builtins.schedStealCount()` sums every P's steal counter, so
// `steals=` is a DIRECT reading of the mechanism rather than an inference from
// timing.
//
// Determinism: each child's result is a pure function of its index, and the
// aggregate is an order-independent sum, so `aggregate=` is identical however
// many processors serviced the work. The driver asserts that invariance across
// MAXON_MAX_PROCS in {1, 2, 7, 12} — which is what makes a stolen thread having
// run EXACTLY ONCE a checked property rather than a hope.
//
// ⛔⛔ **SINCE EC10 IT READS `steals=0 workers=1` AT EVERY PROCESSOR COUNT, AND
// THAT IS THE MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A FAILURE.** An `async f(...)` call creates
// a COROUTINE of the green thread that called it, not a green thread: it is
// published only to its owner's coroutine queue, never to a P ring or the global
// queue, so `__sched_wake_or_spawn` is never called, no worker M is ever created,
// and there is nothing anywhere for a thief to find. `producer` still creates all
// of the work and `aggregate` is still the proof that every child ran exactly
// once — what changed is that the work never leaves the M its owner is running on.
//
// The parent-commit reading of this same program, same box, same binary shape:
// `workers=1 steals=0` at N=1, `2 / 24` at N=2, `7 / 3997` at N=7 and `8 / 3996`
// at N=12. `maxon-shv2/track0/pin-matrix.sh` is the driver that takes both.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE ASSERTION FLIPS BACK WHEN `spawn` LANDS.** A `spawn` primitive
// (`SERVICES_DESIGN.md:62-160`, *"Send is a MOVE"*) creates real green threads,
// which are exactly what W212's ring, its stealing and its worker loop schedule —
// all still built, none of it deleted. That rung re-points this program's
// `producer` at `spawn` and restores `steals > 0`; until then the zero is what a
// correct scheduler answers.

typealias Integer = int(i64.min to i64.max)
typealias IntPromise = Promise with Integer
typealias IntPromiseArray = Array with IntPromise

// Sized so the backlog outlives the time a worker M needs to be created and to
// reach its first steal: a few thousand children, each a short pure-integer
// spin. Deliberately NOT allocation-heavy — that is `alloc-torture`'s subject,
// and mixing the two would make a failure ambiguous between the allocator and
// the scheduler.
let childCount = 4000
let childWork = 400

// The modulus that keeps each child's contribution small and index-dependent.
// Coprime with nothing in particular; it exists so the checksum changes if a
// child runs twice or not at all, which a constant contribution could not show.
let childMixModulus = 7

function child(idx Integer, work Integer) returns Integer
	// CPU-parallel checkpoint: marks this hand-written CPU-bound function as a
	// legitimate `async` target (satisfies the E3073 yielding contract) without
	// claiming fake I/O. No-op at runtime.
	__Builtins.parallelBoundary()

	var acc = 0
	var j = 0

	while j < work 'spin'
		acc = acc + ((idx + j) mod childMixModulus)
		j = j + 1
	end 'spin'

	// The +1 is what makes the aggregate count the children as well as sum them:
	// a child that never ran removes a 1 the driver's fixed expectation includes.
	return acc + 1
end 'child'

// The single producer. Everything it spawns enters the ring of whichever P is
// running IT, which is the whole point — a second P can only get work by taking
// it from this one.
function producer(count Integer, work Integer) returns Integer
	__Builtins.parallelBoundary()

	var promises = IntPromiseArray.create()

	// Spawn phase: queue every child before awaiting any, so the backlog is real
	// and a thief has something to find.
	for i in 0 upto count 'spawn'
		promises.push(async child(i, work: work))
	end 'spawn'

	// Await phase: collect in spawn order. Each result is index-derived and the
	// accumulation order is fixed, so the total is fully deterministic even
	// though the children ran on an arbitrary set of processors.
	var total = 0

	for p in promises 'await'
		total = total + await p
	end 'await'

	return total
end 'producer'

function main() returns ExitCode
	let p = async producer(childCount, work: childWork)
	let total = await p

	let steals = __Builtins.schedStealCount()
	let workers = __Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()
	let cpus = __Builtins.cpuCount()

	// The driver parses these four lines. `aggregate` is the core-count-invariant
	// determinism signal (and the proof every child ran exactly once); `steals`
	// is the direct reading of the mechanism this program exists for; `workers`
	// proves more than one M went live; `cpucount` gives the driver ncpu.
	print("aggregate={total}\n")
	print("steals={steals}\n")
	print("workers={workers}\n")
	print("cpucount={cpus}\n")

	// Fixed success ExitCode: deterministic across runs (so the exit-code
	// identity assertion holds) and never 101 (the leak-check gate value).
	return 42
end 'main'
